:: day-1_scene_rowan_desire [nobr]
<h3>The Question</h3>

<p>The fire is low. The wine is almost gone. Rowan is still on the floor, lute silent in their lap, their body warm against the side of the bed where I'm sitting. The conversation has been about goats and barons and magistrates. Normal things. Safe things.</p>

<p>I break the safety.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"Rowan. When I'm... different. When I have a woman's body. Will you still—" I can't finish. The sentence has too many possible endings and all of them are terrifying.</div>

<p>Rowan looks at me. Their honey eyes catch the firelight and hold it. They're quiet for a long time — not because they don't know the answer but because they're choosing how to say it.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ROWAN</span>"You're asking if I'll still want you."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"I'm asking if—" I gesture. At my body. At the broad shoulders and flat chest and the cock I'm sitting on. "If this is what you want, and this is going to be gone, then—"</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ROWAN</span>"Edrin." They set the lute aside. Turn to face me fully. Cross-legged on the floor, close enough that their knee touches my calf. "I've been wanting you since I was sixteen and you choked on a candied apple and I thought it was the most endearing thing I'd ever seen. I wanted you when you were a gangly teenager with bad hair. I wanted you when you grew into a man with a sharp jaw and sad eyes. And I will want you when you're a woman with those same eyes in a different face."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"You don't know what the body will look like."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ROWAN</span>"I don't care what the body looks like." Their voice is firm. Certain. The rare moments when Rowan stops performing and speaks from the bedrock. "I've fucked people with cocks and people with cunts and people who had both and people who had neither and the thing that makes me want someone has never, ever been about what's between their legs. It's about—" They tap their chest. Heart. "This. And yours hasn't changed. It can't change. The Rite rewrites bodies. It doesn't rewrite souls."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"But the sex. The sex will be—"</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ROWAN</span>"Different. Wonderfully, terrifyingly different." They lean forward. "Edrin. I once spent a night with a woman who had been a man. She told me the orgasms were different — not better, not worse, different. Deeper, she said. Less concentrated. Like the pleasure goes through you instead of shooting out of you. She said it was like learning a new instrument — the music is still music, but the technique is completely different."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"That's — that's exactly what I'm afraid of."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ROWAN</span>"I know. And it's exactly what I'm excited about." They grin — that specific Rowan grin, the one that's tender and filthy simultaneously. "I'm a bard. I live for new instruments. New sounds. New ways to make someone sing."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"Sing?"</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ROWAN</span>"Moan. Scream. Whimper. The whole register. I want to learn yours. Whatever yours turns out to be."</div>

<p>The warmth that floods through me isn't arousal — or it is, but it's bigger than arousal. It's the feeling of being wanted not despite the change but through it. Of someone looking at the transformation not as loss but as possibility. New territory. New music. New ways to make someone come.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"Rowan."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ROWAN</span>"Mmhm?"</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"If I have breasts — when I have breasts — are you going to stare at them?"</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ROWAN</span>"Absolutely. Shamelessly. I'll write a sonnet about each one."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"There'll only be two."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ROWAN</span>"I'll write a duet."</div>

<p>I laugh. The sound is genuine and surprised and it comes from the same deep place as the fear, and somehow both can exist simultaneously — the terror of what's coming and the warmth of being wanted through it.</p>

<p>Rowan stays. They play. The music is softer now — intimate, low, the kind of thing you play for one person in a room and no one else. The fire dies. The darkness grows.</p>

<p>They don't touch me. Not tonight. Tonight the intimacy is verbal — the confession of desire across the threshold of transformation, the promise that the body on the other side will be met with hunger, not horror.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ROWAN</span>"I'll be here," they say at the door. "On the other side. And I'll want you. Whatever shape you're in."</div>

